408
00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:39.000
If Bob is in denial, how many patients do you have?

409
00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:42.000
One, two, or three, or four.

410
00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:43.000
Bob is in denial.

411
00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:44.000
How many patients do you have?

412
00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:46.000
One, two, or three, or four?

413
00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:47.000
One.

414
00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:50.000
Bob is dependent.

415
00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:52.000
How many patients do you have?

416
00:21:52.000 --> 00:21:53.000
One or two?

417
00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:57.000
Two because you've got to get the codependent.

418
00:21:57.000 --> 00:21:58.000
Bob is a manipulator.

419
00:21:58.000 --> 00:21:59.000
How many patients do you have?

420
00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:00.000
One or two?

421
00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:01.000
One.

422
00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:02.000
One.

423
00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:03.000
Do you see what I'm saying?

424
00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:06.000
Because you don't have to do anything with the person who's being manipulated

425
00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:08.000
because there's no self-esteem issues.

426
00:22:08.000 --> 00:22:10.000
So with denial, you've got one patient.

427
00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:12.000
With dependency, you have two patients.

428
00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:14.000
With manipulation, you have one patient.

429
00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:16.000
Does that make sense?

430
00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:17.000
Okay.

431
00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:21.000
Let's talk now about things specific to alcoholism.

432
00:22:21.000 --> 00:22:26.000
Let's talk about Wernicke's or Corsicos.

433
00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:29.000
Wernicke's and Corsicos.

434
00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:33.000
Typically, they are separate, but Bortz often mumps them together.

435
00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:38.000
Wernicke's is an encephalopathy and Corsicos is a psychosis,

436
00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:40.000
but they tend to go together.

437
00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:43.000
You find them in the same patient.

438
00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:50.000
So Wernicke Corsicos is number one psychosis induced by vitamin B1

439
00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:53.000
or thiamin deficiency.

440
00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:56.000
This is a scenario where you lose touch with reality.

441
00:22:56.000 --> 00:23:01.000
You go insane because you don't have B1.

442
00:23:01.000 --> 00:23:02.000
What's it do?

443
00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:09.000
Psychosis, psychosis, which means insanity or loss of touch with reality.

444
00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:15.000
These are psychotic people, not just a little bit emotionally disturbed.

445
00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:18.000
These are psychotics.

446
00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:23.000
The primary symptom number two is amnesia with confabulation.

447
00:23:23.000 --> 00:23:26.000
Amnesia with confabulation.

448
00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:28.000
Amnesia means memory loss.

449
00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:30.000
Confabulation means making up stories.

450
00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:33.000
Why do they make up stories?

451
00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:36.000
Because they forgot.

452
00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:40.000
Well, in that case, then I'm psychotic.

453
00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:46.000
Because I often make up details that I have forgotten.

454
00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:47.000
Right?

455
00:23:47.000 --> 00:23:51.000
So then why are they psychotic and me not?

456
00:23:51.000 --> 00:23:53.000
Fair question. Why?

457
00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:57.000
So everybody that forgets something and makes up a story

458
00:23:57.000 --> 00:24:00.000
to fill in what they forgot is insane and psychotic.

459
00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.000
True or false?

460
00:24:02.000 --> 00:24:03.000
False.

461
00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:04.000
Well, then explain to me the difference.

462
00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:05.000
Why?

463
00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:10.000
Why are these poor people called in psychotic and we're not?

464
00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:13.000
Because they believe it.

465
00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:19.000
See, when I fill in details, because I forgot, I know it's not really true.

466
00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:23.000
But they really believe it.

467
00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:29.000
The lie is just as real as reality.

468
00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:32.000
And their memory loss is not,

469
00:24:32.000 --> 00:24:35.000
oh, I forgot what I did last night when I was drunk.

470
00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:41.000
No, it is what happened to the 90s.

471
00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:43.000
They don't remember anything in the whole 1990s.

472
00:24:43.000 --> 00:24:47.000
They'll lose entire decades of their memory.

473
00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:49.000
So what will they do?

474
00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:53.000
Make up stories as to what they were doing during the 90s.

475
00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:56.000
I had a guy that with workies,

476
00:24:56.000 --> 00:25:01.000
he was Ronald Reagan's national security advisor during the 80s.

477
00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:05.000
The guy had never made it past third grade.

478
00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:08.000
Now, whatever you thought of Reagan,

479
00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:12.000
he wasn't that bad that he would have had a third grader running the national security.

480
00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:13.000
You know what I'm saying?

481
00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:16.000
So he definitely was never Reagan's national security advisor.

482
00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:17.000
He thought he was.

483
00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:21.000
He could tell you meetings, dates, times, people.

484
00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:30.000
He had this whole psychotic reality built around Ronald Reagan's terms in office.

485
00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:32.000
And he believed it.

486
00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:33.000
He literally believed it.

487
00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:38.000
He believed it as that was as real as anything that was happening right then and right there.

488
00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:41.000
So how do you deal with that?

489
00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:42.000
How do you think you deal with that?

490
00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:50.000
You've got a Wernicke-Sporza-Koskai who thinks he is Obama's secretary of defense.

491
00:25:50.000 --> 00:25:55.000
And so he's got to get up and go to a cabinet meeting right now.

492
00:25:55.000 --> 00:25:59.000
How do you deal with that?

493
00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:03.000
What would probably be a bad way to deal with that?

494
00:26:03.000 --> 00:26:06.000
What's that?

495
00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:10.000
Okay, we don't usually agree or disagree.

496
00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:13.000
We don't confront why.

497
00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:16.000
Why?

498
00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:20.000
Right.

499
00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:23.000
And it's due to brain damage.

500
00:26:23.000 --> 00:26:26.000
So are they ever going to learn what's true and what's real?

501
00:26:26.000 --> 00:26:28.000
So is this permanent or not?

502
00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:30.000
Typically permanent.

503
00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:34.000
So you don't present reality because they can't learn it.

504
00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:37.000
So what do you do?

505
00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:39.000
See, this is the questions they ask you.

506
00:26:39.000 --> 00:26:44.000
They give you a person with Wernicke's and they say that Wernicke says he's going to a presidential cabinet meeting at 8 o'clock this morning.

507
00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:46.000
How do you deal with that?

508
00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:47.000
Distract.

509
00:26:47.000 --> 00:26:50.000
Okay, distract is not as good a word as redirect.

510
00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:52.000
Redirect is a really good word.

511
00:26:52.000 --> 00:26:59.000
And that means to take what he's all about that you can't do and rechannel it into something he can do.

512
00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:04.000
So can he go to Barack Obama's cabinet meeting this morning?

513
00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:05.000
No.

514
00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:08.000
So what you do is you say something like this.

515
00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:19.000
Well, why don't we go, why don't you then get a shower, when you're done we'll go watch CNN to see what the news of the day is in Washington, D.C.

516
00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:21.000
You see that?

517
00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:23.000
Is he going to do that?

518
00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:25.000
Probably because he, you see what I'm saying?

519
00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:33.000
And you don't get into a fight with him about no, you're Joe the milkman, you're not Ronald Reagan's national security advisor.

520
00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:34.000
